<template webc:type="11ty" 11ty:type="md">
## Approach 2: Chunk the table into multiple tables

Alternatively, you could have a separate table for each league. This approach would work great for situations where there are multiple data points for each row and a list like the one created in the first example wouldn't work well. There are a few things to be aware of if using this approach:

- Each table will need its own header cells since header cells will not work across tables.
- If you wanted to put in the effort with styling, it would be possible to build separate tables that would visually look like a single table. To do so, you would need the following:

    - Create a table with just the header cells
    -  Use a heading tag (such as a `<h2>`,not a header cell) for each league
    - On the table row element containing the header cells for each league, apply a screen reader only class so the headers are visually hidden but still announced by screen readers. I recommend the technique for absolutely positioning content off-screen from the [Invisible content just for screen reader article from WebAIM.](https://webaim.org/techniques/css/invisiblecontent/).

## Conclusion

Tables can be a challenge to build with accessibility in mind, but with some foethought, planning, and sometimes a little creativity it's ppossible to build tables that screen reader users can take full advantage of. Hopefully this resource can give you a few idea for how to approach building a table accessibly.
</template>